A||N||6||VISCERAL||New big swaybar source info||||1||
Z||000000||VISCERAL||11-28-2001||02:30 PM||||On the yahoo fiero racer forum one guy takes late model camaro/TA sway bars which is hollow and 1.25" and takes an oxy-acetylin torch to them on the bends and reshapes them to match the fiero's swaybar profile. They're bigger, lighter and stiffer than stock. All around winner. Another guy mounts them not under the rear cradle but over them, with collars and heim joints. You gain more ground clearance than the standard method. I believe they(camaro/ta) might also have a 1" bar that we can adapt. Will do research unless someone in a warmer climate could do it, it will take me a while.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||11-28-2001||03:24 PM||||I just got rid of an 89 Firebird GTA (or are you referring to the even later model?) parts car. I looked at the front sway bar before the junk man hauled it off and it was a BIG bar. It was also very wide. It would be difficult for me to believe it could be reshaped to fit a Fiero... front or rear (you weren't real specific there). Besides, don't you want the bar in the front to be bigger then the rear?<P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P><B>JUST ADDED: </B>1988 GT w/ T-Tops, 5-Speed, and Performance Sound.||||reg||1
Z||000002||VISCERAL||11-28-2001||03:31 PM||||1993-up late model front bar-hollowed, yes you do want a bigger bar on the front, but some people might want a bigger one on the back like 3800 or v8 owners.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||11-28-2001||06:13 PM||||Doesn't heating the bar remove the torsional strength of it?||||reg||1
Z||000004||VISCERAL||11-28-2001||07:55 PM||||Any engineers to validate the adverse heat theory? Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jefrysuko||11-28-2001||07:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by VISCERAL:<BR><B>Any engineers to validate the adverse heat theory? Anyone?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Don't know yet.  I am in that class next semester so i'll have to get back to you on that one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||shark93726||11-28-2001||08:18 PM||||Yes, heating any type of spring steel (high carbon) will lose the temper. It would have to be re-tempered by heating to proper temperature and then rapid quenching in oil.<P>However, if the heated part is past the straight part, (on the bent ends) it may not adversely affect the strength, because that part does not twist, and is probably thick enough not to flex much.||||reg||1
